      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] At 5:42 AM on a Tuesday in November, Martin Kowalski stood at the edge of Bay Four in a Lorain, Ohio stamping plant, watching cold rain streak the high clerestory windows. [BEAT] The air inside smelled of hydraulic fluid, hot-rolled steel, and burnt vending-machine coffee. Beneath his boots, the concrete slab hummed with the idle vibration of twelve-hundred-ton presses waiting for the morning shift. Martin was fifty-four, a former tool-and-die maker who had spent the last eleven years as an independent industrial safety trainer. His routine never varied: arrive forty minutes early, test the projector, clip on the lavalier microphone, and shake hands with the floor lead before the whistle blew. [PAUSE 1.5s] This morning, however, there was no auditorium. Management had ordered the briefing held right on the mezzanine beside Press Four, because Press Four had logged three near-misses and one crushed thumb in the past six months. [EMPHASIS: forty pairs of steel-toed boots] were already clattering up the iron stairs, their owners tired, half-awake, and glaring at the rolling sixty-inch monitor parked beside Martin's folding table. Martin tapped his laptop trackpad. The screen glowed, blinked, and spat a jagged purple line across his opening slide\u2014a neat bar chart of OSHA recordables. [LOWER VOICE] The plant's overhead bridge crane rumbled past, and his wireless mic receiver flashed a frantic red light, then went dark. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SPEED UP] Martin smiled tightly and reached for the HDMI cable. He unplugged it, blew into the port, and jammed it back in. Nothing. The rolling monitor stayed stubbornly blue, reading NO SIGNAL in blocky white letters. [BEAT] Across the concrete, Ray Delgado\u2014the third-shift floor lead, arms crossed over a grease-stained Carhartt jacket\u2014let out a short, dry bark of a laugh. \"Hey, clipboard,\" Ray called out over the hiss of an air line. \"You got ten minutes before line start. If your cartoon show is broke, just hand us the sign-in sheet and let us get to work.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] A ripple of low chuckles rolled through the operators. Martin felt heat climb the back of his neck. He tried the backup dongle in his briefcase. Still blue. He tapped the lapel mic clipped to his collar, leaned toward the portable PA speaker, and heard only a hollow, rhythmic pop-pop-pop\u2014electromagnetic interference from the crane's busbar. [LEAN IN] \"Give me thirty seconds, Ray,\" Martin said, raising his voice just enough to carry. \"The monitor's just\u2014\" \"The monitor's been busted since July,\" Ray cut him off, not unkindly, just flat. \"And nobody here wants to stare at pie charts at six in the morning anyway.\" [EMPHASIS: the deadest silence in manufacturing] settled over the mezzanine\u2014not quiet, because a factory is never quiet, but the heavy, impenetrable silence of forty people who had already decided not to listen.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [LOWER VOICE] Martin stood behind his folding table, his thumb resting on the cold edge of his laptop. For a second, exhaustion hit him square in the chest. He had driven two hours through sleet from Toledo. He had spent until midnight updating those slides with the plant's own incident numbers, hoping data would finally break through the pride of a veteran crew. [BEAT] Now the data was trapped inside a black rectangle, and his voice was too small for a room with forty-foot ceilings. He thought about packing up, blaming the plant's AV gear in his post-visit report, and rescheduling for next week. [SLOW DOWN] Nobody would blame him. The contract even had a clause for facility readiness. [PAUSE 2s] Then Martin looked past Ray's shoulder at Press Four itself. The massive green frame towered twenty feet into the gloom, its die space open, its yellow light-curtain stanchions bolted to the floor. Someone had hung a faded rag over the left sensor bracket\u2014just a shop towel, draped there out of habit. [STILLNESS] Martin stared at that rag. His slides didn't have a photo of that rag. His wireless mic couldn't fix that rag. If he walked out now, Press Four would run another eight-hour shift with a blind spot on its left flank.\n\n[WARM SMILE] [EMPHASIS: no slides, no mic, no safety net]\u2014Martin unclipped the dead lapel mic and dropped it into his shirt pocket. He shut the laptop lid with a crisp snap. Then he stepped around the folding table, walked past the blue monitor, and strode straight down the two iron steps toward the yellow safety line painted beside Press Four. [PAUSE 1.5s] The operators watched him, confused. Ray uncrossed his arms. \"Where you going, man?\" Ray asked. Martin stopped two feet from the press, turned to face the crew, and planted his boots wide. He didn't shout. He used the flat, carrying baritone he had learned in thirty years of loud shops. \"Ray's right,\" Martin said. \"You don't need pie charts. You need all ten fingers.\" [LEAN IN] He pointed behind him at the green steel frame. \"Who runs Four on first shift?\" A young operator in the second row raised a gloved hand. Martin nodded to him. \"Walk me through your reach-in. Not the manual version. The real one, when the parts bin is sticking.\" The young man glanced at Ray, then stepped forward. \"I hit the palm buttons, wait for the ram, reach in left-handed.\" Martin pointed at the faded shop towel draped over the light-curtain bracket. \"And when that rag slips two inches down?\" [PAUSE 2s] Nobody answered. Ray walked over, yanked the rag off the bracket, and stared at the dusty lens underneath. \"That's been there since Tuesday,\" Ray muttered. \"We put it there to keep coolant spray off the housing.\" \"I know why you put it there,\" Martin said gently. \"And [EMPHASIS: the machine does not care about PowerPoint]. It only cares whether that beam breaks before the ram drops.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] For the next twelve minutes, Martin never looked back at the mezzanine. He used a piece of yellow soapstone from his pocket to mark the exact pinch point on the die shoe\u2014[EMPHASIS: three inches of scarred steel] where a previous operator had lost his thumb. He had Ray stand at the palm buttons while the younger operator tested the light curtain with a wooden push-stick, timing the stop-response by ear. [BEAT] By 6:08 AM, the forty operators weren't leaning against the rail anymore. They had formed a tight semicircle around Press Four, trading fixes on how to mount a proper splash shield without blocking the sensor. When the shift whistle finally blew, Ray walked Martin back up the iron stairs to the folding table. Ray picked up the dead HDMI cable, coiled it neatly, and set it beside Martin's closed laptop. \"Same time next month?\" Ray asked. Martin zipped his briefcase and nodded. \"Same time,\" he said. \"Leave the monitor off.\" [PAUSE 2s] [STILLNESS] Walking out into the cold Lorain rain, Martin realized his throat was a little hoarse, his boots were greased, and he hadn't shown a single slide\u2014and for the first time in eleven years, he knew the room had actually heard him.",
