      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] On the Tuesday before Thanksgiving, freezing rain hammered the concourse windows at Minneapolis-Saint Paul International Airport like handfuls of gravel. Inside Gate Twelve, the air smelled of wet wool, burnt espresso, and the sharp, static-charged heat of too many bodies packed into too few rows of vinyl chairs. [BEAT] Dolores Vance\u2014everyone on Concourse B called her Dee\u2014stood behind the laminate podium with her reading glasses perched on the bridge of her nose. Twenty-four years with the airline had taught her to read a boarding area the way a sailor reads a barometer. [LOWER VOICE] Tonight, the pressure was dropping fast. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nOne hundred and ninety passengers were ticketed for Flight 418 to Denver. Outside, the de-icing trucks crawled across the tarmac with their amber beacons pulsing through the sleet. Above Dee's head, the twin departure monitors flickered, refreshed, and then froze on a departure time that had already passed forty minutes ago. [EMPHASIS: The blue spinning wheel of death] sat dead center on her gate terminal, turning lazily over a manifest she could no longer print. Around the seating area, phones chimed in ragged waves as the airline's mobile app pushed three different gate-change alerts in four minutes, each one contradicting the last. People were standing now, rolling carry-ons into the aisle, craning their necks toward the podium as if Dee were hiding the airplane behind her ankles. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SPEED UP] Dee picked up the wired handset of the gate microphone to make a holding announcement. She pressed the rubber thumb-switch. Nothing. No hum, no click, no tinny echo from the ceiling speakers. She tapped the grille twice. Dead. [BEAT] Across the podium, Kyle Mercer, a twenty-six-year-old operations supervisor in a crisp reflective vest, was crouched beside the server cabinet with a flashlight clenched between his teeth. \"Give me two minutes, Dee,\" Kyle said around the barrel of the light. \"IT is pushing a remote patch to the concourse switch. Once it reboots, the screens and the PA will come right back up.\" [SLOW DOWN] Dee looked out at the crowd. A tall man in a camel-hair coat was already marching toward the desk, his boarding pass held like a subpoena. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\n\"Excuse me,\" the man said, loud enough for the first six rows to hear. His name, Dee saw from his luggage tag, was Arthur Pendelton. \"My phone says we're boarding at Gate Nineteen. The board behind you says we left at six-fifteen. Nobody has said a word into a microphone in half an hour. Are we flying to Denver tonight or not?\" [BEAT] Before Dee could answer, a woman beside him held up her screen. \"Mine says cancelled!\" she called out. \"It literally says cancelled!\" [SPEED UP] A murmur rippled backward through the crowd, sharp and contagious. Someone near the charging station muttered about missed connections. A toddler began to wail. Kyle popped up from behind the cabinet, his face flushed. \"Folks, please, our system is just refreshing!\" he shouted over the noise, his voice cracking slightly on the last word. \"Just check your app for the most accurate updates!\" [LOWER VOICE] Dee closed her eyes for half a second. [EMPHASIS: Nobody believes a blinking cursor] when they are cold, tired, and lied to by a glowing rectangle. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [LOWER VOICE] By seven-thirty, the remote patch had failed twice. The gate terminal went completely black, then rebooted into a diagnostic screen of white text on a cobalt background. Kyle was on his cell phone with the help desk in Dallas, pacing behind the luggage sizer, repeating the same serial number into the receiver. Out in the seating area, the crowd had stopped sitting. One hundred and ninety people stood shoulder-to-shoulder from the jetbridge door all the way to the Hudson News kiosk, blocking the main concourse walkway. [BEAT] Dee felt the familiar ache behind her ribs\u2014the late-shift exhaustion that made every fluorescent tube overhead buzz like a hornet. For a moment, she doubted herself. She was sixty-one years old, two years from retirement, standing behind a dead computer in a storm she could not control. What could one voice without a microphone possibly do against a wall of smartphone alerts and rising panic? [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\n[LEAN IN] Then Dee remembered the back hallway behind the jetbridge\u2014the cramped crew-scheduling alcove where the old-timers used to post standby lists before the monitors were installed. [BEAT] She tapped Kyle on the shoulder. \"Keep calling Dallas,\" she said quietly. \"I'm going to get the board.\" Kyle blinked, confused, as Dee slipped through the badge-only door beside the podium. Three minutes later, she wheeled out a heavy, double-sided rolling slate chalkboard on squeaky casters. The oak frame was scarred with decades of baggage-cart dents, and the bottom tray held two stubs of yellow railroad chalk and a felt eraser worn down to the wood. [STILLNESS] [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] Dee did not roll the chalkboard behind the podium. She rolled it straight out into the open space in front of the jetbridge door, right into the center of the crowd. [SLOW DOWN] The squeak of the casters cut through the chatter. A few people stepped back, surprised. Dee picked up the stub of yellow chalk, turned her back to the room, and pressed it firmly against the slate. *Scritch, scritch, scritch.* [BEAT] In the sudden hush, the sound of chalk on slate carried all the way to the newsstand. At the top, in tall, blocky capital letters, she wrote: FLIGHT 418 \u2014 DENVER. Below it, she drew a horizontal line and wrote three short lines: PLANE: AT GATE. CREW: ON BOARD. DE-ICING: 25 MINS. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nShe turned around. Arthur Pendelton was staring at the board, his mouth slightly open. \"Wait,\" he said, his voice dropping to match the room. \"The plane is actually here?\" [WARM SMILE] Dee nodded once. \"It's sitting forty feet behind that window, Mr. Pendelton,\" she said, pitching her voice just loud enough for the front rows to carry it back. \"The pilots are in the cockpit. The flight attendants are warming the cabin. The only thing between us and Denver is a truck spraying hot glycol on the wings, and a computer in Dallas that forgot how to tell you that.\" [BEAT] [EMPHASIS: The room leaned forward.] People in the back stood on tiptoe to read the yellow letters. Someone snapped a photo of the chalkboard\u2014not to complain, but to text it to their spouse. \"What about the app saying cancelled?\" the woman with the phone asked, softer now. Dee turned back to the slate, drew a small box at the bottom, and wrote: IGNORE THE APP. TRUST THE CHALK. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] For the next forty minutes, Gate Twelve ran on yellow dust and human voice. Every ten minutes, Dee walked out to the slate, erased one number, and wrote the next. *DE-ICING: 15 MINS.* *DE-ICING: 5 MINS.* When a passenger asked about tight connections in Denver, Dee didn't point them to a kiosk; she wrote CONNECTIONS: SEE DEE AT PODIUM in the corner and worked through the list by hand with a pen and a paper manifest. [BEAT] Kyle finally came out from behind the desk, his phone in his pocket, watching in quiet astonishment as one hundred and ninety strangers formed an orderly line without a single announcement over the PA. [LOWER VOICE] [EMPHASIS: Clarity is louder than amplification.] [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nAt eight-twenty, the monitors overhead suddenly blinked back to life with a cheerful chime, displaying the wrong gate number all over again. Nobody looked up. They were watching Dolores Vance pick up the felt eraser, wipe the slate clean, and write three final words in bold yellow chalk: NOW BOARDING. WELCOME. [STILLNESS] [PAUSE 2s] A quiet wave of applause started near the window and rolled through the concourse as the jetbridge door swung open.",
