      "annotatedScript": "At four-fifteen in the morning on a Tuesday in November, Marta Kowalski stood in the back of her bakery on Fulton Street, listening to the hiss of the steam-injected deck oven. [PAUSE 1.5s] Outside, Lake Michigan wind rattled the loading-bay door, carrying the first wet sleet of the season. Inside, the air smelled of toasted caraway, malt, and damp wool. Marta was fifty-four, with knuckles permanently dusted white by rye flour and a habit of checking the crust color of every third loaf with the back of her thumb. [BEAT] Her grandfather had started Kowalski Hearth & Rye with two brick ovens and a delivery van that only ran in second gear. Over twelve years, Marta had built it into a forty-two-store regional supplier. [SLOW DOWN] That was the number she kept taped above the office desk: [EMPHASIS: forty-two stores].\n\nThree days before the Great Lakes Independent Grocers Summit, the email had arrived from Harbortown Markets, the chain that accounted for sixty-eight percent of her weekly volume. [LOWER VOICE] Effective December first, they were consolidating their artisan bread program under a national frozen-dough distributor. No call. Just a PDF signed by a procurement director she had never met. [PAUSE 1.5s] If Marta did not replace that volume within thirty days, she would have to lay off eleven bakers before Christmas. Her only shot was a twelve-minute slot at the summit's Buyer Showcase\u2014a room full of independent co-op managers and regional grocers who had already sat through fourteen pitches before lunch.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] By ten-thirty that morning, Marta was standing in the service corridor behind Ballroom C at the Amway Grand. Her nephew, Tomasz, who handled the bakery's spreadsheets and social media, was frantically tapping the side of a borrowed laptop. [SPEED UP] \"Aunt Marta, the HDMI adapter isn't talking to the hotel's switcher,\" Tomasz whispered, his face pale under the fluorescent lights. \"The AV guy says he's got two other rooms blowing up right now. He told us to just plug in and pray.\" Marta looked down at the two linen-wrapped loaves of seeded sourdough rye she had brought in a wicker basket\u2014baked at five a.m., still faintly warm through the cloth. [BEAT] \"We have forty slides, Tomasz,\" she said evenly. \"Cost-per-unit tables, shelf-life graphs, margin projections. They won't listen to a baker without numbers.\"\n\n\"They're calling you in two minutes,\" Tomasz said. \"If the deck doesn't load, what do you want me to do?\" Marta adjusted the cheap plastic lapel mic clipped to her collar. \"Load it anyway,\" she told him. \"Just get me on the screen.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] Ballroom C was cold, cavernous, and half-asleep. Roughly eighty buyers sat at round tables littered with coffee cups and crumpled napkins. A few were scrolling through their phones. Marta walked to the lectern, cleared her throat, and clicked the remote. [PAUSE 1.5s] Behind her, the massive twenty-foot projection screen flickered\u2014and then froze on a bright, merciless rectangle of Windows blue. A spinning white wheel sat in the center like a tiny, mocking clock. [STILLNESS] Marta clicked again. Nothing. She tapped the spacebar. The laptop fan roared to life, loud enough to catch the lapel mic, which let out a sharp, metallic screech\u2014and then went completely dead. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[LOWER VOICE] No slides. No microphone. Eighty pairs of eyes suddenly looking up from their phones, waiting to see how fast she would crumble. In the front row, a buyer in a charcoal suit\u2014Gerald Vance, from the Traverse City co-op\u2014glanced at his watch and let out a quiet, unmistakable sigh. [BEAT] Marta felt the heat rise in her neck. For three seconds, the only sound in Ballroom C was the hum of the ceiling vents. She thought about the eleven bakers waiting back on Fulton Street. She thought about the PDF. Every instinct told her to apologize, to ask the AV tech for five more minutes, to stall until the screen came back.\n\n[LEAN IN] Instead, Marta reached down, unclipped the dead lapel mic, and set it gently on the lectern. [PAUSE 1.5s] She picked up the wicker basket, walked around the side of the podium, and stepped right to the edge of the low stage. [SLOW DOWN] \"Good morning,\" she said, pitching her voice to the back wall the way she used to shout over the dough mixers. [EMPHASIS: \"My name is Marta Kowalski, and my computer just died.\"] A few people in the third row blinked. Nobody laughed, but nobody looked back down at their phones either.\n\n\"I had forty slides for you,\" Marta said, lifting the linen cloth off the basket. \"Margin charts. Freight schedules. All of it. [BEAT] And if you want a spreadsheet, my nephew Tomasz is standing by the back door with a thumb drive and a very sweaty forehead.\" A small ripple of chuckles moved across the left side of the room. Gerald Vance in the front row didn't smile, but he stopped looking at his watch. \"What I actually brought,\" Marta continued, holding up one of the dark, blistered rye loaves, \"was baked five hours ago in Grand Rapids. [EMPHASIS: You can't fake a crust.]\"\n\n[SPEED UP] She didn't stay on the stage. She stepped down the three wooden stairs into the aisle, carrying the basket, and walked straight to Gerald Vance's table. She tore the loaf in half with her bare hands\u2014a sharp, audible crack that echoed through the quiet room\u2014and set the steaming halves on a paper napkin in the middle of the table. \"Smell it,\" she said softly. \"Caraway, fermented rye, and eighteen hours of cold-proofing. Harbortown Markets just dropped us for frozen dough from Ohio. [PAUSE 1.5s] Frozen dough doesn't crack like that.\"\n\n[WARM SMILE] Vance stared at the bread for a beat, then broke off a piece of the crumb. He chewed slowly. Around him, three other buyers at the table reached for the loaf. Marta moved to the next table, tearing the second loaf into quarters, talking while she walked\u2014no notes, no laser pointer, just the plain math she knew by heart. She told them the exact wholesale price, the delivery window, the forty-eight-hour freshness guarantee. [BEAT] When someone in the back asked about shrink rates, she didn't hedge. \"Three percent if you display it near the deli,\" she called back. \"Seven percent if you hide it by the cereal.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] By the time she reached the center aisle, the ballroom didn't feel cold anymore. People were leaning across tables, passing pieces of rye, asking questions about minimum orders and Tuesday-morning drop-offs. [SLOW DOWN] When the moderator finally tapped his watch at the twelve-minute mark, the projector behind the stage was still frozen on that same blue screen. [PAUSE 1.5s] Nobody had looked at it once.\n\n[STILLNESS] Marta walked back to the lectern, picked up her dead microphone, and handed it to the moderator with a quiet nod. [LOWER VOICE] That afternoon, Gerald Vance signed a twelve-store trial order on the back of a coffee-stained agenda. By Friday, four other independent grocers from the room had called the bakery on Fulton Street. [PAUSE 2s] They didn't replace all forty-two stores overnight\u2014but they saved every single job in that kitchen. And to this day, Tomasz still brings a thumb drive to every pitch, just in case. [WARM SMILE] But Marta never walks into a ballroom without a warm loaf of rye.",
