      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] On a Tuesday morning in late October, Caleb Halvorsen stood behind the heavy velvet curtain of Ballroom A at the Boise Centre, watching a stagehand tape down a thick black power cable. [PAUSE 1.5s] Caleb was fifty-four years old, a wildlife tracker and search-and-rescue instructor out of Missoula, Montana, with knuckles scarred from barbed wire and a voice that carried the dry, unhurried cadence of someone used to talking over river water. [BEAT] Outside the convention center, an early autumn sleet was tapping against the glass atrium, turning the Boise streets slick and gray. Inside, four hundred and fifty county sheriffs, dispatch supervisors, and backcountry coordinators were finishing their coffee, waiting for the closing keynote of the Mountain West Emergency Management Symposium. [PAUSE 1.5s] Caleb had spent three weeks building his deck. He had forty-two slides: high-resolution GIS terrain overlays, thermal drone loops from a November search in the Sawtooths, and side-by-side photographs of boot prints in frost. He wanted to prove to this room that modern search-and-rescue had entered a new era of precision. [EMPHASIS: He had never trusted a room full of strangers without his data.] His hands were steady in the timber, but here, under the humming halogen cans of a hotel ballroom, his collar felt half a size too tight. At nine-fifteen, the program chair introduced him, the applause rose and settled, and Caleb walked out to the lectern. He tapped the spacebar on the house laptop. [BEAT] Nothing happened. The forty-foot projection screen behind him stayed dark. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SPEED UP] Caleb clicked the spacebar twice more. In the back of the room, a technician in a black headset leaned over a glowing mixing board, his fingers flying across a bank of faders. \"Give it three seconds, Mr. Halvorsen,\" the tech called out, his voice echoing off the acoustic panels. \"The HDMI switcher is handshaking.\" [BEAT] Caleb nodded, smiled at the front row, and launched into his opening line anyway. Five words in, the wireless lavalier clipped to his lapel let out a sharp, metallic pop\u2014[EMPHASIS: and died.] [PAUSE 2s] Not a fade. Not static. Just an abrupt, hollow silence that swallowed his sentence mid-air. In the third row, a county commissioner from Twin Falls lowered his coffee cup. Beside the lectern, the backup monitor flickered once, flashed a green diagnostic bar, and went [EMPHASIS: the color of wet slate.] The stagehand jogged up the side stairs, handed Caleb a handheld corded mic, and whispered, \"Switch is dead. No slides. Just talk.\" Caleb pressed the switch on the handheld mic. Dead too. The house rack had tripped a breaker. [PAUSE 1.5s] \"We're working on it!\" the tech shouted from the back, already unscrewing a panel. Across the ballroom, chairs creaked. A dozen phones lit up as people checked their emails. The air in the room turned thin and polite\u2014the unmistakable temperature of an audience deciding the next forty minutes were going to be a wash. [BEAT]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [LOWER VOICE] Caleb stood behind the oak lectern, gripping the dead handheld microphone like a wrench. For ten seconds, he did nothing. His throat went dry. He thought about the three weeks he had spent cropping thermal stills in Missoula, the hours he had spent rehearsing transitions so he wouldn't look like an amateur in front of people who ran multi-agency budgets. [PAUSE 1.5s] He could hear the tech swearing softly into his headset at the back of the hall. He could see the program chair near the exit doors, checking her watch with a tight, apologetic grimace. [EMPHASIS: Every instinct told him to wait for the machine.] If he waited, he could blame the venue. Nobody would fault him for a dead breaker. Nobody would remember him, either. [BEAT] Then Caleb looked down at his own boots\u2014scuffed leather, still carrying a faint rim of dried mud from a ridge walk four days earlier\u2014and he remembered a November afternoon three winters ago in the Bitterroot Range. [PAUSE 2s] He had been called in at dusk to find a sixty-two-year-old bowhunter who had walked off a logging spur in a freezing mist. The man's phone was dead. The thermal helicopter was grounded by ceiling. Every digital tool on the mountain had been useless. [SLOW DOWN] Caleb set the dead microphone down on the lectern. He didn't tap it. He didn't make a joke about technology. He simply stepped out from behind the oak box, walked down the three carpeted steps into the center aisle, and stopped between the fourth and fifth rows. [STILLNESS] [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [LEAN IN] \"You don't need a screen to see a trail,\" Caleb said. His voice wasn't loud, just pitched low and flat so it carried under the ceiling rafters. \"And you don't need a microphone if the room is quiet.\" [BEAT] A few heads turned. The phones in the third row clicked dark. \"Three years ago, up near Lost Horse Creek,\" Caleb said, \"we had a hunter missing for nineteen hours. Snow was coming in at four thousand feet. My GPS couldn't hold a lock under the canopy, and the radio repeater was iced over.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] He crouched slightly in the aisle, his palm hovering three inches above the patterned hotel carpet. \"When the screen goes dark, you do not stare at a dead screen,\" he told them. \"You look at the ground.\" [EMPHASIS: The room leaned in before he raised his voice.] \"A man walking in fog doesn't leave a boot print on granite,\" Caleb said, his fingers pinching the air as if holding something smaller than a matchstick. \"He leaves a [EMPHASIS: quarter-inch snap on a serviceberry stem.] He leaves three larch needles bent against the grain of the wind. He leaves a wet smudge on the underside of a shale flake where his heel slipped.\" [BEAT] From the fifth row, a sheriff's deputy in a tan uniform leaned forward, resting his elbows on his knees. \"How do you know it's not a deer?\" the deputy asked. [WARM SMILE] Caleb looked straight at him. \"A deer clips the stem clean at an angle,\" Caleb said. \"A boot crushes the bark first, then breaks it. You can see the white pith bruised on the uphill side.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] He stood back up, walking slowly up the aisle toward the back of the room, turning his head left and right as if reading the carpet. \"Every person in this room runs a team that wants better gear,\" he said. \"Better drones. Better overlays. And I love that gear. [SLOW DOWN] But gear only tells you where someone was. [EMPHASIS: Observation tells you who they are.]\" [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [STILLNESS] For the next thirty-five minutes, nobody touched the breaker. The technician at the back of the hall quietly set his screwdriver down on the mixing board and sat on his flight case to listen. [BEAT] Caleb walked the entire length of Ballroom A, using the chairs as ridge lines, the aisle as a creek bed, and the exit signs as false summits. He showed them how a lost hiker's stride shortens by four inches when panic sets in\u2014how the toes turn outward when the quads begin to burn. [PAUSE 1.5s] He didn't have a single slide. He didn't have a single chart. Yet by the time he reached the back row and turned to face the stage, four hundred and fifty people were twisted in their seats, tracking his hands as if they were watching a live rescue unfold on the carpet. [SLOW DOWN] When he finished, he didn't ask for questions. He simply nodded once, walked back up the aisle, and picked up his clicker from the lectern. [PAUSE 2s] The room didn't erupt right away. There was a full beat of silence\u2014the kind of silence you only get in the timber right after a heavy snow stops falling. Then the deputy in the fifth row began to clap, and the rest of the ballroom followed. [EMPHASIS: Later, in the lobby, nobody asked him for his slide deck.] They asked him how to look at the ground.",
