      "annotatedScript": "[STILLNESS] [LOWER VOICE] In November of 2019,Callum MacLeod stood behind the heavy velvet curtain of the Halifax Marriott Harbourfront Ballroom, staring at a small plastic clicker in his right hand. [BEAT] Outside the double-paned windows, freezing sleet ticked against the glass, and the harbor water below was the color of wet slate. Inside, three hundred offshore superintendents, marine surveyors, and commercial divers were scraping chairs against the carpet, waiting for the morning keynote of the Atlantic Subsea Safety Symposium. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nCallum was forty-six years old, broad-shouldered from twenty years in drysuits, with a permanent squint around his eyes and knuckles scarred by barnacles and slag. He was not a professional speaker. For fifteen years, his office had been forty feet below the surface of the Bay of Fundy, welding cracked tidal turbine struts in [EMPHASIS: zero visibility]. Down there, the silt hung so thick in the water that a diver's helmet lamp could not penetrate six inches. You worked by memory, by the hum of the arc, and by the feel of the electrode against cold steel. [BEAT] When the symposium committee asked him to deliver the opening address on catastrophic structural fatigue, he had spent three weeks building a forty-slide deck full of high-resolution sonar scans, stress-fracture diagrams, and underwater inspection logs. He had rehearsed every transition until he knew the exact second each chart would appear behind him.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SPEED UP] At 8:30 sharp, the conference chair introduced him, the applause rose, and Callum walked out to the lectern. He clipped the wireless lavalier to his flannel collar, tapped the spacebar on the house laptop, and smiled. [PAUSE 1.5s] Nothing happened. The twenty-foot projection screen behind him stayed stubbornly blue, displaying a floating white box that read: NO SIGNAL DETECTED. [BEAT] Callum pressed the clicker again. A faint static pop cracked through the ceiling speakers, and then the lavalier microphone died completely. Three hundred faces looked up at him in the sudden, ringing quiet.\n\nFrom the back of the room, a young AV technician in a black hoodie sprinted toward the stage, waving both hands. \"Give me two minutes!\" the technician whispered loudly, dropping to his knees behind the lectern to yank at a tangle of HDMI cables. \"The switcher box is rebooting. Just stall them!\" [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nCallum looked out at the ballroom. In the front row, two senior surveyors from St. John's were already checking their phones. Near the center aisle, a table of riggers muttered to one another, pouring coffee. [LOWER VOICE] A cold knot tightened in Callum's stomach. Without the sonar scans, his entire argument about micro-fractures on the Bay of Fundy turbines seemed invisible. He tried speaking without the mic, raising his voice over the hum of the HVAC system. \"Good morning,\" he called out. \"While we sort out the\u2014\" [BEAT] His voice bounced flatly off the acoustic tiles, thin and strained. Half the back row did not even look up. The technician's wrench clinked against a metal floor box, and someone near the exit coughed.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] By 8:34, four minutes had bled away\u2014an eternity on a keynote stage. The technician stood up, his face flushed, holding a frayed adapter cable. \"I'm sorry, man,\" he hissed. \"The main projector bulb blew when the switcher surged, and the backup mic channel is locked out. We have to pull the plug and reschedule your slot for after lunch.\" [PAUSE 2s]\n\nCallum stared at the dead blue screen, then down at his laminated cue cards, which were useless without the slides. Every instinct told him to nod, step off the stage, and let the committee fix the mess. He was a welder, not an entertainer. If the gear was broken, you aborted the dive. [BEAT] Yet as he looked at the back row, he saw a younger diver in a faded company jacket\u2014someone who had driven six hours through sleet from Cape Breton just to hear how a crew survived a strut failure in the dark. If Callum walked off now, that kid would only learn that when the lights go out, you quit.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [STILLNESS] Callum reached up, unclipped the dead lavalier, and set it gently on the lectern. He picked up his cue cards, placed them face down, and stepped three paces out from behind the wooden podium until he was standing at the very edge of the stage, directly under the house lights. [PAUSE 2s] He did not shout. He waited. One second. Two seconds. Three. [LOWER VOICE] The chatter in the middle aisle began to thin. A surveyor in the front row pocketed his phone. The silence grew heavy enough that everyone in the room could hear the sleet tapping the harbor windows.\n\n\"In the Bay of Fundy,\" Callum said, his voice pitched low and steady, carrying cleanly because the room had gone dead quiet to hear him, \"the tide moves faster than a man can swim. When you drop down to a turbine strut at slack water, you have [EMPHASIS: twenty-two minutes] before the current rips you off the steel.\" [LEAN IN] He held up his left hand, fingers curled as if gripping a cold flange. \"And you cannot see your own hand in front of your faceplate. Not an inch. Your helmet light just turns the silt into a white wall.\"\n\n[WARM SMILE] A rigger in the third row slowly set his coffee cup down. Callum took another step toward the edge of the stage. \"So when a trainee asks me how we weld a three-inch crack we cannot see,\" Callum continued, \"I tell them the truth. [EMPHASIS: You do not need to see the arc to trust the bead.] You listen to the sizzle. You feel the drag of the rod through your wrist. You count the seconds in your head, and you know the shape of the work before you ever strike the metal.\" [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nFor the next twelve minutes, Callum never once looked back at the dead screen. Without a single slide, he walked the ballroom through the night a twelve-ton turbine strut sheared forty feet under the bay. He traced the hairline fracture in the air with his thumb. He mimicked the muffled crack of the underwater torch, the sudden drop in water temperature, and the exact rhythm of a blind root pass. [SPEED UP] Whenever a door opened in the back, he paused for half a beat until the room pulled him back in. By the time he described surfacing into the sleet with a completed weld, three hundred people were leaning forward in their chairs, completely still.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] When Callum finished, he did not say thank you right away. He simply stood at the edge of the stage, his scarred hands resting at his sides, and let the last sentence settle into the carpet. [PAUSE 2s] For two full seconds, nobody moved. Then the front row stood up, followed by the riggers in the middle, and the applause rolled through the hall louder than any sound system could have carried.\n\nLater that afternoon, the conference chair found Callum by the coat check, zipping his parka. \"We got the projector fixed for the afternoon,\" the chair said, shaking his head. \"Do you want to run your slides for the closing panel?\" [BEAT] Callum looked back toward the ballroom doors, smiled once, and shook his head. \"No,\" he said. \"They already saw it.\" [STILLNESS] Outside, the sleet had turned to clear rain, and Callum walked down toward the harbor, knowing that the best work he had ever done had happened after the lights went out.",
