      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] On the third Thursday of November, a cold drizzle settled over Worcester, Massachusetts, turning the slate roofs of four rival parishes the color of wet coal. [PAUSE 1.5s] Marek Zielinski stood in the cramped vestry of St. Jude\u2019s with a canvas tool roll under his arm and a thermos of black coffee going lukewarm in his coat pocket. He was fifty-four, a municipal horologist by trade, with calluses shaped like brass gears and the patient, slightly stooped posture of a man who spent his life inside narrow staircases. [BEAT] For six weeks, Marek had been hired by the Worcester Civic Centennial Committee to do something no one in the city had managed since nineteen twenty-four: synchronize the noon strike across [EMPHASIS: four rival spires]\u2014St. Jude\u2019s, Trinity Chapel, Our Lady of Sorrows, and First Congregational\u2014all within half a mile of City Hall, all proud, all stubborn, and all convinced their own clock ran truest.\n\nAt eleven-forty, the centennial broadcast crew was already strung across the civic green below, microphones wrapped in plastic bags, cameras pointed skyward. Inside St. Jude\u2019s, Marek\u2019s job looked simple on paper. He had wired a wireless GPS master relay into the vestry wall, linked to three slave receivers in the neighboring towers, and projected a live diagnostic dashboard onto a portable screen so the four parish sextons could watch the countdown together. [WARM SMILE] That was the theory, anyway. In practice, the four sextons stood in a tight semicircle around his folding table like men waiting to witness a duel.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SPEED UP] \"My tower\u2019s thirty-two milliseconds ahead of Trinity,\" said Father Brennan of Our Lady of Sorrows, squinting at the glowing green numbers on Marek\u2019s screen. \"Your box is dragging us down.\"\n\n\"Your tower\u2019s a quarter-tone flat in November,\" shot back Arthur Pendelton from First Congregational, folding his arms. \"The damp swells your oak frame. Don\u2019t blame the box.\"\n\n[LEAN IN] Marek didn\u2019t look up from his multimeter. \"Gentlemen, the GPS locks at eleven-fifty. Nobody\u2019s oak frame is arguing with a satellite.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] At eleven-forty-eight, the wireless relay blinked amber. Then it blinked red. Then the portable diagnostic screen\u2014the one showing every parish their precious millisecond alignment\u2014froze on a gray error bar and went black. [BEAT] A second later, the handheld VHF radio Marek had clipped to his belt let out a thin, wet squelch and died.\n\n\"What happened?\" Brennan demanded, stepping closer.\n\n\"Power surge on the vestry circuit?\" Pendelton asked, already looking toward the door. \"We told the committee this electronic nonsense wouldn\u2019t hold in a hundred-year-old tower.\"\n\n[LOWER VOICE] Marek checked the breaker. The breaker was fine. He checked the antenna lead. Moisture had crept into the coax coupling somewhere up in the louvers, shorting the transmitter board. With eleven minutes to noon, [EMPHASIS: the room went dead]\u2014no dashboard, no radio link, no automated master pulse. Below them, three hundred people were gathering on the wet grass of the civic green, and the local radio truck had already opened its live mic.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] For ninety seconds, Marek felt the particular coldness that every technician knows: the sensation of four pairs of eyes deciding you are a fraud. [PAUSE 1.5s] His fingers trembled slightly as he unscrewed the relay housing, hoping for a blown fuse he could bridge with a paperclip. There was no fuse. Just a scorched surface-mount resistor smelling faintly of burnt sugar. [BEAT] He looked at the four sextons. They weren\u2019t looking at him anymore; they were looking at their wristwatches, already preparing their excuses for why their parish wouldn\u2019t be the one to look foolish at noon.\n\nMarek closed the tool roll. [STILLNESS] [PAUSE 2s] He realized, with a sudden, almost absurd clarity, that if he kept staring at the dead console, he would lose the next ten minutes to a post-mortem nobody wanted. He needed to leave the box behind.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] \"Gentlemen,\" Marek said, his voice dropping low enough that they had to stop talking to hear him. [LOWER VOICE] \"The electronics are gone. We\u2019re not fixing a circuit board in nine minutes.\"\n\nPendelton scoffed. \"Then call the committee and tell them it\u2019s off.\"\n\n\"No,\" Marek said, stepping away from the folding table and into the center of the stone floor. [LEAN IN] \"You four have been ringing these bells by hand for thirty years. How far is Trinity\u2019s belfry from here?\"\n\n\"Four hundred yards,\" said the Trinity sexton, a quiet woman named Ruth Morales.\n\n\"And you can see St. Jude\u2019s spire from your east louver?\"\n\n\"Every morning,\" Ruth said.\n\n[SPEED UP] Marek pulled a heavy brass pocket watch from his vest\u2014not a digital timer, just an old railroad-grade Hamilton he kept for checking pendulum beats\u2014and laid it on the table. \"Forget the satellites. Forget the dashboard. At eleven-fifty-nine, I am going up to the roof parapet of St. Jude\u2019s with a white canvas drop cloth. When I drop that cloth, I\u2019m pulling our tenor bell.\" He pointed at Ruth. \"You\u2019re four hundred yards away. Sound takes a little over a second to reach you, so don\u2019t [EMPHASIS: listen for the iron]\u2014watch the cloth. The instant it drops, you pull.\" He turned to Brennan and Pendelton. \"You two can\u2019t see my roof, but you can see Trinity\u2019s west window. Ruth, you\u2019ll have your lantern in the west louver. When you pull, you flash it twice. They\u2019ll see the light before they hear the bronze.\"\n\nBrennan blinked. \"You want us to run a visual relay across four churches in the rain?\"\n\n\"I want you to stop watching my broken screen and watch each other,\" Marek said. [WARM SMILE] \"You\u2019ve got seven minutes to get to your ropes.\"\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [STILLNESS] At eleven-fifty-eight, Marek stood on the slick slate parapet of St. Jude\u2019s, wind whipping his coat, the white canvas drop cloth bunched in his left hand. Below, the civic green was a sea of umbrellas. He couldn\u2019t hear the radio announcer, couldn\u2019t see the diagnostic numbers, had no way to know if Brennan had even made it back to Our Lady of Sorrows in time. [PAUSE 2s] He held his Hamilton watch in his right palm, watching the sweep hand climb toward sixty.\n\nTen seconds. Five. [SLOW DOWN] Three. Two. [BEAT] Marek let go of the white cloth. It snapped downward against the dark stone.\n\nHe didn\u2019t wait to see if Trinity saw it. He grabbed the hemp rope, leaned his whole weight into the pull, and felt the two-ton tenor bell roll over its crown. [EMPHASIS: Twelve beats of silence] seemed to stretch between the tug of the rope and the first great bronze wave that rolled out over the wet roofs of Worcester. [PAUSE 1.5s] One second later, from four hundred yards east, Trinity\u2019s bell answered\u2014not a millisecond late, not a quarter-tone flat, just a clean, heavy harmonic that locked into St. Jude\u2019s decay. Half a heartbeat after that, Our Lady of Sorrows and First Congregational struck together from across the green, four separate towers folding into a single chord that rattled the rainwater off the gutters.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [WARM SMILE] Down on the grass, nobody clapped at first. They just stood under their umbrellas, looking up at the four spires as the twelve strokes rolled across the city, one after another, held together by [EMPHASIS: not a single wire]\u2014only four people watching each other through the rain. [PAUSE 2s] When Marek finally climbed down to the vestry, the dead diagnostic screen was still sitting on the folding table, gray and useless. Nobody asked about it. Arthur Pendelton was already pouring coffee from Marek\u2019s thermos into four paper cups, and for the first time in a century, nobody was arguing about whose clock ran truest.",
